# Historical GPU implementation review

> Findings below describe the previous monolithic runner. Resolved/current
> behavior is assessed in `master.plan` and documented in `manual.md`.

The feature/vm/gpu branch automates the core host-
preparation, verification, attachment, rollback, and
detach workflow. It does not automate every item in the
supplied 12-step procedure, and several steps are
intentionally implemented differently.

The feature contract test passes, and the worktree
remains unchanged.

## Important review findings

1. macOS + NVIDIA is not rejected.
   The runner accepts --profile macos without detecting
   the GPU vendor. It could therefore attach an NVIDIA
   GPU to macOS even though the supplied procedure says
   to stop at planning. See setup/vm/gpu.sh:151 and
   setup/vm/gpu.sh:815.

2. Optional blacklisting does not select the matching
   vendor.
   --blacklist-host-drivers blacklists both AMD and
   NVIDIA drivers globally, including additional NVIDIA
   modules. That differs from the supplied “use only the
   matching branch” instruction and can affect unrelated
   host GPUs. It is protected by explicit display-loss
   confirmation. See setup/vm/gpu.sh:529 and docs/setup/
   vm/gpu/manual.md:12.

3. VM host autostart is not disabled or checked.
   The runner defaults to not starting the VM immediately
   and offers opt-in --start, but it never inspects or
   changes the VM’s onboot setting. An existing onboot: 1
   therefore remains enabled. See setup/vm/gpu.sh:862.

4. GPU/audio identity is inferred, not verified.
   The user provides one PCI BDF. The runner
   automatically includes every function sharing that
   slot, but does not confirm class 0300 for GPU or 0403
   for audio, does not require an audio function, and
   does not use vendor/device IDs. See setup/vm/
   gpu.sh:201 and setup/vm/gpu.sh:223.

5. A successful --test is procedural, not an enforced
   attach prerequisite.
   attach repeats important checks—VM stopped, IOMMU
   isolation, managed host state, and vfio-pci binding—
   but it does not require evidence of an earlier
   successful --test or repeat every test check. See
   setup/vm/gpu.sh:671 versus setup/vm/gpu.sh:806.

## Step-by-step coverage

 Supplied step                  Feature coverage
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━  ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
 1. Record hardware values      Partial. Requires
                                <GPU_BDF>; discovers all
                                sibling functions
                                automatically.
                                <AUDIO_BDF>, <GPU_ID>,
                                and <AUDIO_ID> are not
                                inputs.
─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────
 2. Firmware/prerequisites      Mostly manual. BIOS/UEFI
                                remains manual. It does
                                not install cpu-checker
                                or PVE headers and does
                                not run kvm-ok, dmesg,
                                x2apic, or AMD-Vi
                                checks. It detects
                                Intel/AMD from /proc/
                                cpuinfo.
─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────
 3. Identify GPU/groups         Mostly automated through
                                sysfs. Discovers
                                functions, requires
                                IOMMU groups, rejects
                                unrelated endpoints,
                                detects boot_vga, and
                                checks VM assignment
                                conflicts. It does not
                                run the listed lspci
                                inspection or verify
                                GPU/audio classes. It
                                does not configure ACS
                                override.
─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────
 4. Enable IOMMU                Automated with a
                                different file strategy.
                                Adds the CPU-specific
                                IOMMU flag and iommu=pt.
                                GRUB uses a managed
                                drop-in; systemd-boot
                                uses /etc/kernel/
                                cmdline. It does not
                                edit the single
                                assignment in /etc/
                                default/grub, add ACS
                                override, or support
                                iommu=soft.
─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────
 5. VFIO modules                Automated. Writes a
                                managed modules-load.d
                                file with the three
                                current VFIO modules and
                                conditionally includes
                                vfio_virqfd when
                                applicable.
─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────
 6. Blacklist host drivers      Optional and materially
                                different. When
                                requested, globally
                                blacklists both AMD and
                                NVIDIA stacks rather
                                than selecting the
                                passthrough GPU’s
                                vendor.
─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────
 7. Bind functions              Automated using a safer
                                alternative. Uses exact-
                                BDF driver_override
                                binding in initramfs
                                instead of reusable
                                vendor/device IDs. It
                                intentionally does not
                                add unsafe interrupts or
                                kvm ignore_msrs=1.
─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────
 8. Rebuild/reboot              Automated. Runs update-
                                grub or proxmox-boot-
                                tool refresh, then
                                update-initramfs -u -k
                                all. Reboot is opt-in
                                and requires --yes.
─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────
 9. Post-reboot verification    Mostly automated. Checks
                                active kernel flags,
                                managed files, IOMMU
                                grouping, VFIO modules,
                                exact-BDF configuration,
                                vfio-pci ownership, and
                                VM compatibility. It
                                does not inspect dmesg
                                or verbose lspci output.
─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────
 10. Attach to VM               Automated. Requires the
                                VM to already be
                                stopped, validates Q35
                                and desktop OVMF/EFI
                                requirements, rejects
                                HA-managed VMs, selects
                                a free hostpciN, and
                                attaches the complete
                                slot including audio.
                                Desktop profiles add
                                pcie=1,x-vga=1 and set
                                vga: none. It does not
                                disable onboot.
─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────
 11. Guest-specific work        Not automated. Profiles
                                influence VM
                                configuration, but guest
                                drivers and guest
                                verification remain
                                manual. No OpenCore/
                                Monterey validation and
                                no NVIDIA/macOS
                                prohibition exist.
─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────
 12. Edge cases                 Mostly intentionally
                                excluded. No ACS
                                override, iommu=soft,
                                hugepages, unsafe
                                interrupts, ROM
                                handling, or reset
                                patches. The runner
                                refuses running VMs
                                instead of force-
                                stopping them and
                                supplies managed detach/
                                unprepare rollback.

The exclusions are explicitly documented in docs/setup/
vm/gpu/manual.md:7, including ACS override, guest
drivers, ROMs, and reset patches.

## Validation coverage

The automated repository test confirms:

- AMD CPU flag selection
- multifunction GPU/audio discovery
- one isolated IOMMU group
- exact-BDF binding without capturing an identical second
  GPU

- guarded global blacklisting
- VFIO readiness
- Q35/OVMF macOS attachment
- safe hostpci allocation
- detach and unprepare rollback

It does not test Intel, NVIDIA-specific behavior,
Windows/Linux/compute profiles, systemd-boot, separate
valid IOMMU groups, boot-VGA handling, PVE 6 behavior, or
real hardware. The synthetic contract is in actions/
validate.vm.gpu.sh:50.
